(43567, 574, 689, '<td valign="top">I just wanted to answer a few of the comments that were made about this photo. First of all, unless I ask for another tornado, I cant move buildings out of the picture! Part of Architecture is location, so the buildings around it are part of the picture too. That is one reason you can''t see all of the building. If you crop out the building behind, you miss much of the detail of this amazing structure. It is a downtown building, so it is surrounded by large buildings on all sides.  I wish the lighting was different on the front, but unless I was able to set up a lot of strobes, it would never happen. Also, shooting from the ground would not have granted a better view of the entire building and the angels would have appeared distored and the lighting would have been odd on a night shot.</td>', 'I just wanted to answer a few of the comments that were made about this photo. First of all, unless I ask for another tornado, I cant move buildings out of the picture! Part of Architecture is location, so the buildings around it are part of the picture too. That is one reason you can''t see all of the building. If you crop out the building behind, you miss much of the detail of this amazing structure. It is a downtown building, so it is surrounded by large buildings on all sides.  I wish the lighting was different on the front, but unless I was able to set up a lot of strobes, it would never happen. Also, shooting from the ground would not have granted a better view of the entire building and the angels would have appeared distored and the lighting would have been odd on a night shot.', '2002-04-14 23:02:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(43568, 575, 352, '<td valign="top">From the shadows, it looks like this was taken closer to mid-day, Does this shot improve by taking it early in the day?  At this time the bright sky makes the picture look over-exposed.  The horizon is also tilted slightly to the right.</td>', 'From the shadows, it looks like this was taken closer to mid-day, Does this shot improve by taking it early in the day?  At this time the bright sky makes the picture look over-exposed.  The horizon is also tilted slightly to the right.', '2002-04-01 01:36:00.000000', NULL, 1),
